\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Gauge-coupled cochains and neighboring lattice operators}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Becher and Joos give a complex-cochain gauge coupling in
\citet{§3.5, p. 358, (3.59)–(3.64)}{becher1982dirac}.
Their forward difference in (3.60) is already
#{\rho(U_i(x))^{-1}f(x+\varepsilon_i)-f(x)}, agreeing with the
[based coefficient difference](fdip-00SH) under the same link
convention. Their p. 359 discussion (a)–(d) limits
this construction to the simplest external-gauge coupling and
leaves gauge-invariant flavor separation open. It therefore
does not identify every interacting component of this
cochain space with a separate spinor.}

\p{Catterall uses matrix-valued cells in a supersymmetric gauge
theory in dimension #{2}; see
\citet{§4, pp. 7–8, (4.1)–(4.4)}{catterall2004geometrical}.
Gauge transformations act at both endpoints.
In the notation here, such a cell coefficient #{\Phi_I(x)}
has a law of the form
#{\Phi_I(x)\mapsto g(x)\Phi_I(x)g(x+\sum_{i\in I}\varepsilon_i)^{-1}}.
The source complexifies the fields and distinguishes the
opposite orientation by its conjugate transformation law.
This differs from the single-base action
#{\alpha_I(x)\mapsto\rho(g(x))\alpha_I(x)}. A comparison
requires a specified path transport and a coefficient map;
endpoint notation alone supplies neither.}

\p{Catterall, Laiho and Unmuth-Yockey use uncoupled oriented
simplicial incidence in
\citet{§3, pp. 3–5, (3.1)–(3.7)}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}.
Their skew-adjoint Dirac–Kähler sign translates to
#{\extd -\extdag=\cov}, whereas the self-adjoint operator here
is #{\extD=\extd+\extdag}. The parity count in
\ref{fdip-00T0} is compatible with incidence counting, but
does not import an anomaly calculation into a coupled model.}

\p{The [centered covariant operator](fdip-009O) and its
[Wilson modification](fdip-009U) act on site spinors with the
pairings and coefficient fields stated there. The present
operator acts on all cellular degrees and is defined over a
commutative ring. Its strict kernel reductions in
\ref{fdip-00SY} and \ref{fdip-00SZ} are calculations for that
operator; equality of component counts would not establish an
intertwiner with either site-spinor operator.}
